From 483839770b819f1a5dbd08666a4672055e341998 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Javier Maestro <jjmaestro@ieee.org>
Date: Sun, 26 Jul 2026 03:08:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 13/14] feat: support a matrix of archives per version

Some archives are published as a set of files per version rather than
as a single file: a toolchain publishes one archive per (target triple,
component) and all of them together make up the toolchain. The index
can only describe one archive per (version, source), so those have to
be spread over one repo per component, losing the shared version, the
shared sources and the shared integrity bookkeeping.

Add an optional `matrix` to the index: an ordered list of axes, each an
object with a `key` (the context property the axis sets) and `values`,
either a list of values or a map of value to the extra context
properties that apply when that value is selected. The version is
downloaded once per combination of the axes, in matrix order and with
the last axis varying fastest, and the integrities are nested by axis
value, in the same order, with the same "global" or per-source leaf as
a regular version property.

The combination's properties join the materialized repo context right
after the default context, so the `url` and the `strip_prefix` of a
source can reference them, and they are materialized in axis order so
an axis can reference the axes before it.

Add an optional `output` template to lay the combinations out: those
that render the same `output` merge into a single tree, the rest
partition it, and each rendered `output` also gets a target of its own,
so consumers can depend on one partition instead of on the whole tree.

A source is only used if all of its combinations download successfully,
so a partial mirror falls through to the next source instead of leaving
behind a half-materialized tree.

Without a `matrix` there is exactly one combination, with no properties
and no `output`, which is the behavior of every existing index.
